<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scindias</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scindias were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> ruling family who became one of the most powerful houses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-confederacy">Maratha Confederacy</a>, later based at Gwalior. Along with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a>, they held large territories in northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Rivalry among these chiefs weakened central authority after 1740.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scindias.jpg" alt="Scindias" width="196" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xufanc · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gwalior_Coat_of_arms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Chhatrapati at Satara to the Peshwa's administration at Pune, which supervised a confederacy of semi-autonomous chiefs, among them the Gaekwads of Baroda, Holkars of Indore, <b>Scindias</b> of Gwalior and Bhonsles of Nagpur, who collected revenue and maintained armies while acknowledging the Peshwa's leadership and the Chhatrapati's formal sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1730s the Marathas under the Peshwa Baji Rao I and later the <b>Scindia</b> and Holkar houses exacted heavy tribute, nobles feuded among themselves and the treasury was exhausted; in 1810 the young princess Krishna Kumari was poisoned to avert a war between rival suitors from Jaipur…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Damaji Rao II took Baroda (Vadodara) and much of the province, and the Gaekwads became one of the great Maratha houses alongside the Peshwas, Holkars and <b>Scindias</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/baroda-state">Baroda State</a></li><li><blockquote>Dhar was badly weakened by the wars of <b>Scindia</b> and Holkar around 1800 and recovered under British protection after the treaty of 1819.</blockquote><a href="/state/dhar-state">Dhar State</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1806 the British returned Gohad and Gwalior to the <b>Scindias</b> and compensated Rana Kirat Singh with Dholpur, Bari and Rajakhera on the Chambal river.</blockquote><a href="/state/dholpur-state">Dholpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>It paid tribute to the <b>Sindhia</b> rulers of Gwalior State and came under British protection in the early 19th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/khilchipur-state">Khilchipur State</a></li></ul></section>
